## 20. Configurable Item Code and Item Description Rule Engine

### 20.1 Core Requirement

Build a fully configurable, database-driven **Item Code Generation Engine** and **Item Description Generation Engine**.

Neither engine may depend on hardcoded category rules or fixed field sequences in frontend or backend source code.

Authorized administrators must be able to create, edit, validate, test, activate, deactivate, version, and roll back rule configurations through the web application.

Changes to rules must take effect through controlled configuration activation without requiring application code changes or redeployment.

The system must distinguish between:
- Draft configuration.
- Validated configuration.
- Active configuration.
- Retired configuration.
- Historical configuration versions used by existing items.

Only authorized users may manage rules. Rule editing, testing, approval, activation, and rollback must be controlled through RBAC and fully audited.

### 20.2 Rule Administration Screens

Create a dedicated **Item Code & Description Rule Builder** module with these screens:

1. Rule Dashboard.
2. Item Code Rule Builder.
3. Item Description Rule Builder.
4. Category-to-Rule Mapping.
5. Classification Code Mapping.
6. Sequence and Numbering Configuration.
7. Rule Validation and Test Simulator.
8. Rule Version History.
9. Rule Approval and Activation.
10. Rule Audit Log.
11. Rollback and Recovery.

Allow rules to be configured by:
- Organization.
- Item category.
- Item type, where applicable.
- Material or classification combination.
- Branch, only when the business explicitly requires branch-specific numbering or descriptions.
- Effective date and time.
- Rule version.

Support a global default rule with category-specific overrides. Define precedence explicitly, for example:

**Approved category-specific rule → approved organization rule → system default rule.**

Reject ambiguous matches rather than selecting an arbitrary rule.

### 20.3 Configurable Item Code Builder

Provide a visual builder where administrators can construct Item Code formats by arranging components.

Supported component types:
- Fixed text or prefix.
- Category code.
- Material code.
- Type code.
- Material Form code.
- Process code.
- Colour code.
- Manufacturer code, when enabled.
- Other approved classification codes.
- Delimiter or separator.
- Sequential number.
- Optional check digit, if required.

Example configurable pattern:

`{CATEGORY}{MATERIAL}{TYPE}{FORM}{PROCESS}{COLOUR}{SEQUENCE:4}`

Example output:

`FGPPIMBK0001`

The example is illustrative; administrators must be able to change component order, included dimensions, separator rules, sequence width, and applicable categories.

For each component, provide configuration fields such as:
- Component name.
- Source classification or field.
- Enabled/disabled.
- Position/order.
- Fixed value, if applicable.
- Output format.
- Padding length.
- Case conversion.
- Required/optional status.
- Default behavior when a value is missing.
- Validation rule.
- Maximum output length.

Provide conditional logic, such as:
- If Category = FG, include Material, Type, Form, Process, and Colour.
- If Category = RM, use the configured raw-material pattern.
- If Category = SV, use a service-specific pattern.
- If a component is optional, omit it according to the configured policy.
- If a required component is missing, prevent code allocation and display a clear validation error.

Support configurable sequence scope:
- Global.
- Organization.
- Category.
- Configured category combination.
- Branch, only if explicitly enabled by governance.

Sequence allocation must be concurrency-safe and transactionally protected. Use database sequences or atomic counter records with suitable unique constraints. Never use spreadsheet row numbers, browser counters, timestamps, or `MAX(code)+1` as the production sequence mechanism.

Define sequence behavior when a rule changes:
- Continue an existing sequence.
- Start a new sequence namespace.
- Require explicit administrator confirmation before changing sequence scope or reset behavior.

Never reset or reuse issued identifiers without an approved migration policy. Preserve code uniqueness across all applicable scopes.

### 20.4 Configurable Item Description Builder

Create a visual description-template editor using draggable or selectable fields, literals, separators, and conditional segments.

Allow administrators to define a different description template for every category.

Support:
- Field selection and ordering.
- Literal text.
- Separators such as hyphens, spaces, commas, and slashes.
- Conditional fields.
- Optional fields.
- Prefixes and suffixes.
- Text trimming and whitespace normalization.
- Case conversion.
- Controlled lookup values.
- Date or numeric formatting when relevant.
- Length limits.
- Required fields.
- Duplicate-description warnings.
- Category-specific validation.
- Preview and test data.

Initial templates should be supplied as editable defaults:

| Category | Default description pattern |
|---|---|
| FG | Material – Grade (if enabled) – Product Name – Specification – Colour |
| SF | Material – Part Name – Dimensions – Revision (if enabled) |
| RM | Material Name – Grade (if enabled) – Colour – Form – Size |
| BO | Component Name – Manufacturer – Part Number – Rating/Size |
| PM | Packaging Type – Material – Dimensions – Thickness/GSM |
| MA | Equipment/Part Name – Manufacturer – Model/Part No. – Rating/Size |
| CO | Item Name – Brand – Type/Grade – Size |
| SV | Service Name – Scope – Equipment/Reference |
| SC | Material/Part Name – Source/Grade – Colour – Form |
| TR | Product Name – Brand/Manufacturer – Model/Part Number – Key Specification – Size/Rating |
| CP | Equipment Name – Brand/Manufacturer – Model – Capacity/Rating |
| MO | Tool Name – Part/Product – Tool Type – Cavity – Tool ID – Revision |
| HF | Item Name – Brand – Type/Specification – Size/Capacity |

These templates must be stored as configuration records, not embedded as immutable application logic.

Where a category template references a field that is not enabled in the current form, administrators must either enable that field, map the segment to another field, or mark the segment optional. Do not silently generate incomplete or misleading descriptions.

Example template:

`{MATERIAL} - {SPECIFICATION} - {COLOUR} - {DIMENSION}`

Example output:

`PP - Injection Moulded Component - Black - 120 x 80 mm`

The output must be generated from actual user selections and entered values, not from the example literals.

When an optional field is blank, omit the field and its associated separator. Prevent double spaces, leading separators, trailing separators, and duplicate delimiters.

### 20.5 Dynamic Form Configuration

Provide a form configuration layer so administrators can define:
- Fields shown for each category.
- Required versus optional fields.
- Field labels and help text.
- Input type.
- Dropdown master source.
- Validation constraints.
- Field order and grouping.
- Whether a field participates in Item Code generation.
- Whether a field participates in Item Description generation.
- Whether the field is editable after submission.
- Whether the field is editable after approval.

For example, selecting FG may display the fields required by the FG code and description rules, while selecting RM displays the fields configured for RM.

Do not force all categories to use the same form or the same required fields.

Ensure every field referenced by an active rule is mapped to a valid field or classification source. Validate configuration changes before activation.

### 20.6 Rule Simulator and Preview

Before activation, provide a Rule Simulator that allows an administrator to:
1. Select an item category.
2. Select classification values.
3. Enter sample specifications.
4. Generate a preview Item Code.
5. Generate a preview Item Description.
6. See each output component and its source value.
7. Identify missing required values.
8. Check the output against length limits.
9. Test boundary conditions and optional fields.
10. Compare the active rule with the proposed rule.

Show validation messages and explain exactly why a rule succeeds or fails.

The simulator must not permanently allocate a production sequence number or create an SAP item.

Provide automated test cases for every rule version before activation.

### 20.7 Rule Versioning, Approval, and Activation

Every configuration change must create a new immutable version.

Store:
- Rule ID.
- Version number.
- Organization and applicable scope.
- Category and conditions.
- Configuration payload.
- Created by and created timestamp.
- Change reason.
- Validation result.
- Approval decision.
- Approved by and approval timestamp.
- Effective-from timestamp.
- Activation status.
- Previous version reference.

Support a maker-checker process for sensitive rule changes. The creator of a rule change must not approve their own change.

Changes to Item Code format, sequence scope, or uniqueness behavior must require explicit elevated authorization and impact analysis.

Changes to Item Description templates must identify the categories and records affected.

Activation must be atomic: the application must never use a partially saved configuration.

Provide controlled rollback by activating a previous valid version as a new audited configuration event. Do not delete historical versions.

### 20.8 Existing Item Protection

Rules must not silently rewrite existing item identifiers or approved descriptions.

For every created item, preserve:
- The generated Item Code.
- The generated Item Description.
- The Item Code rule ID and version.
- The Description rule ID and version.
- The relevant input snapshot.
- The generation timestamp.
- The rule configuration checksum or immutable version reference.

When rules change:
- New items use the applicable active version.
- Existing drafts follow a configurable migration policy.
- Submitted items retain their submitted snapshot unless formally returned for correction.
- Approved items retain their approved values.
- Already-created SAP ItemCodes must not be changed automatically.

If a description correction is needed after approval, create a controlled change request with approval and SAP synchronization rules.

### 20.9 Configuration Data Model

Add suitable relational tables, such as:

- `item_code_rules`
- `item_code_rule_versions`
- `item_code_rule_components`
- `item_code_sequences`
- `item_description_rules`
- `item_description_rule_versions`
- `item_description_rule_segments`
- `item_category_rule_mappings`
- `item_form_configurations`
- `item_form_field_configurations`
- `rule_validation_runs`
- `rule_test_cases`
- `rule_approval_decisions`
- `rule_activation_history`

The exact normalization can vary, but rule versions must be immutable, auditable, and safely queryable.

Use JSONB only where appropriate for flexible configuration payloads; retain relational constraints for identity, scope, versioning, approval, and sequence integrity.

Never execute arbitrary Python, JavaScript, SQL, shell commands, or user-supplied code from a rule configuration. Implement a safe, declarative rule language with a restricted set of operators, field references, conditions, and formatting functions.

### 20.10 Rule Engine APIs

Implement authorized, versioned endpoints such as:

- `GET /api/v1/rules/item-codes`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes/{id}/versions`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes/{id}/validate`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes/{id}/simulate`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes/{id}/submit`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes/{id}/approve`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-codes/{id}/activate`
- `GET /api/v1/rules/item-descriptions`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-descriptions`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-descriptions/{id}/versions`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-descriptions/{id}/validate`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-descriptions/{id}/simulate`
- `POST /api/v1/rules/item-descriptions/{id}/activate`
- `GET /api/v1/rules/history`

These are suggested endpoint patterns; implement consistent REST conventions and appropriate authorization for each operation.

The normal Item Creation workflow must call the backend rule engine for authoritative validation and output generation. Frontend previews may be used for responsiveness, but the backend remains the source of truth.

### 20.11 Additional Acceptance Criteria

The feature is complete only when:
1. An authorized administrator can change the Item Code pattern without changing application source code.
2. An authorized administrator can change category-specific Item Description templates without redeployment.
3. Administrators can add new categories and map them to new rules.
4. Required fields and classification mappings are validated before rule activation.
5. Code generation remains unique under concurrent requests.
6. Rule changes are versioned and auditable.
7. Rule activation requires the configured approvals.
8. Rollback is supported without deleting historical versions.
9. Existing approved items are not silently rewritten.
10. Branch-specific rules cannot override global rules unless explicitly permitted.
11. A rule cannot execute arbitrary code or unrestricted database queries.
12. The simulator can test proposed rules without consuming production sequence numbers.
13. All rule management APIs enforce backend RBAC.
14. Automated tests cover rule evaluation, conditional logic, missing values, sequence concurrency, version activation, rollback, and authorization.

Implement these capabilities as a first-class configuration module, not as a collection of hardcoded if/else statements.



