- Defines whether the dimension is global, category-specific, hierarchical, code-bearing, description-bearing or validation-only.
- Must distinguish a metadata-only field from a new physical database column. Do not create arbitrary columns without a schema migration and approval.

C.2 Classification Value Management
- Search, filter, create, edit draft, validate, approve, activate, deactivate, retire, import and export classification values.
- Display duplicate checks and parent-child validation.
- Show code/description effects and affected categories before activation.
- Prevent unsafe hard deletion.
- Provide bulk import preview, row-level errors and an import confirmation step.

C.3 Category Configuration
For each category, configure:
- Category code/name/description and active status.
- Applicable classification dimensions.
- Visible, hidden, optional, required, read-only and system-generated fields.
- Field order and grouping on the item creation page.
- Conditional display and validation.
- Default values, where approved.
- Allowed classification values or value filters.
- Item Code rule-set version.
- Item Description rule-set version.
- Required approval level and allowed user scopes.
- SAP mapping/profile, only where verified.
- Effective dates and change history.

C.4 Item Code Rule Builder
Provide a visual, ordered component builder. Each row represents a component and includes:
- Component sequence/order.
- Component name and unique key.
- Source classification dimension/field.
- Source selection: master code, approved alias, fixed literal, transformation output or sequence.
- Applicable categories/conditions.
- Required/optional behavior.
- Conditional inclusion expression from a safe supported rule language.
- Transformation and normalization.
- Fixed width or minimum/maximum width.
- Padding character and alignment.
- Prefix/suffix.
- Separator policy.
- Fallback policy.
- Sequence scope and format.
- Output length impact.
- Uniqueness behavior.
- Error message if resolution fails.
- Active state and rule version.

Allow the administrator to reorder components through explicit controls or drag-and-drop if the UI supports it. Reordering must update a persisted rule version, not merely the client-side view.

C.5 Item Description Rule Builder
Provide a visual, ordered description builder with:
- Component order.
- Source field/dimension.
- Display mode: value name, code, label, approved mapped text or formatted attribute.
- Conditional inclusion.
- Optional/required behavior.
- Prefix/suffix.
- Separator.
- Whitespace/capitalization/normalization.
- Numeric/unit formatting, where appropriate.
- Fallback behavior.
- Maximum description length and truncation policy.
- Duplicate/ambiguity warning.
- Category-specific rule association.
- Rule version and approval status.

Do not silently truncate important specifications. If output exceeds the target limit, show a validation error or use an explicitly approved truncation/shortening strategy. Preserve the complete structured attributes even if a display description has a shorter limit.

C.6 Conditional Rule Configuration
- Configure conditions such as “when Category = RM, Material and Material Form are required”.
- Configure conditions such as “include Colour only when a colour-bearing material or item type is selected”.
- Configure category-specific field visibility and requirements.
- Configure allowed/blocked classification combinations.
- Configure whether a selected value changes the available options for dependent dimensions.
- Configure rule priority and conflict handling.
- Provide a test/simulation interface with sample selections.
- Use a constrained, validated expression/rule engine. Do not execute arbitrary user-supplied Python, JavaScript, SQL or code.
- Detect conflicting, unreachable, circular or ambiguous rules before activation.

The examples above are illustrative only. Implement actual conditions from the approved Kosh classification requirements and current data, and allow authorized administrators to change them through the UI.

C.7 Rule Test Bench / Preview
- Let the administrator select a category and sample values for each applicable dimension.
- Show each resolved Item Code component and the final preview.
- Show each Item Description component and the final preview.
- Show skipped optional components and why they were skipped.
- Show required fields that are missing.
- Show rule source, inherited defaults, overrides and active version.
- Show validation errors and warnings.
- Test uniqueness against persisted records without reserving a production code.
- Clearly distinguish a simulation from permanent code allocation.
- Provide test cases that can be saved with a rule version.

C.8 Rule Approval and Version History
- Draft → Validated → Submitted for Approval → Approved → Scheduled/Active → Retired.
- Support rejection with reason and controlled resubmission.
- Enforce separation of duties where required.
- Show before/after differences, test results, impacted categories and item counts.
- Never activate an unvalidated rule.
- Do not silently change the code/description of already approved items.
- Rule rollback must create an auditable new activation/version rather than deleting history.

D. ITEM CODE GENERATION ENGINE — REQUIRED BEHAVIOR

The Item Code engine must derive codes from active, approved, persisted rule configuration. Do not hardcode the format `FGPPIMBK0001` or any component order into frontend or backend source code. Treat that format only as an illustrative example unless the active configuration defines it.

Support:
- Category-specific code patterns.
- Different patterns for FG, SF, RM, BO, PM, MA, CO, SV, SC, TR, CP, MO, HF and any other approved categories.
- Optional components and conditional components.
- Two-character codes where configured, while allowing per-dimension code lengths and future changes.
- Aliases/mapped code fragments where the source classification code must differ from the code fragment used in the final Item Code.
- Sequence components with configurable width, prefix, padding, reset scope and reset conditions.
- Organization/category/subcategory/plant scope for sequences, only where approved.
- Global uniqueness or other explicitly defined uniqueness scope.
- SAP ItemCode maximum length and character restrictions based on verified SAP Business One metadata/version.
- Validation against inactive/retired classification values.
- Concurrency-safe permanent allocation.
- Provisional preview without consuming a sequence unless the approved policy explicitly allocates at that point.
- A clear distinction between preview, reserved, allocated and synchronized code states.
- Idempotent submission/retry behavior.
- Non-reuse of issued or externally published codes.
- Code reservation expiry only if the approved numbering policy allows it; expiry must not recycle a permanently issued code.
- Safe behavior if approval fails, a request is cancelled or SAP synchronization fails.

Do not use `MAX(ItemCode)+1`, worksheet row numbers, timestamps, browser counters or an in-memory counter for production sequence allocation. Use database sequences or a transactionally locked allocation table with appropriate unique constraints. Verify uniqueness again at allocation/commit time.

If a change to a classification value or rule would produce a different code for an existing item, do not silently rewrite the existing code. Generate an impact report and require an explicit migration/change request, approval and compatibility plan. Existing SAP ItemCodes must never be changed automatically unless the supported SAP process, business policy and explicit approval authorize it.

E. ITEM DESCRIPTION GENERATION ENGINE — REQUIRED BEHAVIOR

Generate Item Description from active, approved, persisted description rules and selected classification/attribute values. The administrator must be able to configure the composition per category and override individual fields/components where authorized.

Support:
- Category-specific descriptions.
- Component order.
- Display names versus codes.
- Optional Grade, Revision, Tool ID and other optional extensions.
- Manufacturer/Brand and model/part number when relevant.
- Dimensions, rating, size, thickness, GSM, cavity, capacity, process, material form, colour and key specification as configured.
- Locale-aware numeric/unit formatting where needed.
- Normalization of whitespace and separators.
- Exclusion of blank/null values without duplicate separators.
- Controlled capitalization.
- Maximum length based on verified destination-field limits.
- Warning for descriptions that become ambiguous when a component is omitted.
- Live preview while the user selects classification values.
- Snapshot of the generated description and rule version at submission/approval.
- Explicit policy for whether an editable manual description is allowed, and if so, who may edit it and how the override is audited.
- Controlled recalculation when a draft changes.
- No silent regeneration of an approved item's description after a rule update.

Keep structured source attributes separate from the generated display description. A generated description is not a substitute for storing the actual material, grade, dimensions, rating or other attributes in their proper fields.

F. PER-TABLE CONFIGURATION MATRIX

Provide a central table showing every classification table/dimension and the rule settings that apply to it. The exact physical table names must be discovered from the actual schema; the labels below are logical dimensions and must not be treated as guaranteed database table names.

For each dimension, expose these settings:
- Dimension key and display label.
- Physical source table/model or metadata reference.
- Primary key and code/name columns, as verified.
- Active status and scope.
- Parent/dependency.
- Categories in which it applies.
- Required/optional/hidden/read-only behavior by category.
- Allowed values and relationship filters.
- Validation pattern, length and uniqueness scope.
- Contribution to Item Code: yes/no/conditional.
- Code fragment source and transformation.
- Component position and separator.
- Contribution to Item Description: yes/no/conditional.
- Description display source and component position.
- Conditional rule association.
- Default/fallback behavior.
- SAP mapping/target field, only if verified.
- Effective rule version and override source.
- Impact count and change history.

The table must allow an administrator to open a dimension and configure its rules without editing source code. Provide both:
1. A dimension-level default configuration.
2. A category + dimension override configuration.

Recommended initial matrix, subject to the actual approved classification schema:

1. Category
- Usually required for item creation.
- Defines the category-specific form, validation profile, Item Code pattern and Item Description pattern.
- Must be selected before category-dependent fields/rules are resolved.
- Category code/name can contribute to Item Code/Description only when the active rule specifies it.
- Category changes after code allocation must trigger impact validation.

2. Subcategory
- Enable only if present in the approved classification model.
- Parent Category relationship must be validated.
- Can filter available types, materials or other dimensions.
- Configure whether its code/name contributes to Item Code and/or Description.

3. Type
- Define category applicability and permitted values.
- Support dependent filtering by Category/Subcategory.
- Configure code fragment, description text, conditional requirement and output position.

4. Material
- Maintain approved material values and their codes/names.
- Configure applicability by category/type.
- Support code/name/mapped alias contribution.
- Validate whether material is required for the selected category.

5. Material Form
- Configure applicable categories/materials and allowed combinations.
- Configure form code and description label independently.
- Allow the field to be optional or required by category/material rule.

6. Process
- Configure allowed process values by category/material/type as required by the actual business taxonomy.
- Support process code mapping, description inclusion and dependency validation.

7. Colour
- Configure applicable categories/materials/types.
- Permit “not applicable” only as an explicit configured state, not as an accidental blank.
- Configure whether colour contributes to Item Code and/or Description.
- Avoid allocating duplicate code fragments to different active colours within the same relevant scope.

8. Grade
- Optional unless a category-specific rule requires it.
- Configure allowed grades by material/category.
- Distinguish actual grade data from a short code fragment used in Item Code.
- Include grade in descriptions only according to the category's active rule.

9. Manufacturer / Brand
- Use the approved centrally maintained master.
- Configure whether manufacturer/brand affects Item Code, Description, duplicate checks or SAP mapping.
- Do not assume it must be embedded in every Item Code.

10. Dimensions and numeric attributes
- Configure data type, unit, precision, minimum/maximum and category applicability.
- Normalize units according to approved rules.
- Include only the configured formatted representation in the generated code/description.
- Preserve original structured measurements and units.

11. Part / Model Number
- Validate according to the category's rules.
- Define whether it is a user-entered field or selected from an approved master.
- Configure uniqueness and whether it appears in code/description.
- Do not transform away meaningful characters without an explicit approved rule.

12. Rating / Size / Thickness / GSM / Cavity / Capacity / Specification
- Treat each as a distinct typed field or approved structured attribute where practical.
- Configure visibility, requirement, unit, precision, validation and category applicability.
- Define exact display format and inclusion conditions.
- Do not concatenate unrelated values into a single field in the database merely to make description generation easier.

13. Revision / Tool ID / other optional extensions
- Do not require them for every category.
- Configure category applicability, permission, validation, code/description contribution and lifecycle behavior.
- If a field is not enabled for a category, do not show it in the creation form or require it in validation.

14. Future administrator-defined dimensions
- Add only through the approved metadata/schema extension mechanism.
- Define data type, validation, category applicability, value source, access policy and code/description behavior.
- If a physical database migration is necessary, generate and review a migration; do not create arbitrary runtime SQL columns.

G. ITEM CREATION FORM MUST BE DRIVEN BY CONFIGURATION

When a user selects a Category:
1. Load the active approved category configuration.
2. Display only the dimensions and fields enabled for that category.
3. Apply the configured field order and grouping.
4. Load only valid values for dependent dimensions.
5. Apply required/optional/hidden/read-only rules.
6. Validate incompatible combinations as the user makes selections.
7. Recalculate the Item Code preview using the active Item Code rule version.
8. Recalculate the Item Description preview using the active Item Description rule version.
9. Explain missing required inputs and the rule that requires them.
10. Show a preview of code components and description components if the user has permission.
11. On server-side validation/submission, resolve the same approved rule version and validate again.
12. Persist the selected classification IDs/keys and relevant rule-version snapshots.
13. If a rule changes while the form is open, detect the version change and ask the user to refresh/revalidate; never silently submit using an unknown mixture of old and new rules.

Do not rely on frontend validation alone. The backend is authoritative for rule resolution, uniqueness, permanent sequence allocation, authorization and approval eligibility.

H. SAFE RULE LANGUAGE AND CONFLICT DETECTION

Do not execute arbitrary SQL, Python, JavaScript or shell commands supplied through the configuration UI. Use a constrained rule schema or approved expression language supporting only defined operators, comparisons, boolean combinations, membership tests, null checks, string/number formatting and controlled references to approved fields.

Before activation, validate:
- Missing or invalid source dimensions.
- Invalid classification codes.
- Duplicate active code fragments where uniqueness is required.
- Cyclic dependencies.
- Conflicting required/hidden/read-only rules.
- Unreachable conditions.
- Ambiguous rule priorities.
- Unknown field references.
- Output-length violations.
- Incompatible SAP destination constraints.
- Sequence-scope collisions.
- Rules that could produce an empty Item Code or invalid Item Description.
- Rules that change an existing code unexpectedly.

Show actionable errors and warnings. A failed validation must prevent activation. Warnings must be categorized and either resolved or explicitly acknowledged by an authorized user under policy.

I. RULE VERSIONING, APPROVAL AND CHANGE IMPACT

Every active rule set must have an immutable version identifier. Capture the rule versions used for:
- Preview (where useful for diagnostics).
- Code reservation/allocation.
- Item submission.
- Approval.
- SAP synchronization.

The exact snapshot policy must be explicit. At minimum, permanently allocated codes and approved descriptions must remain traceable to the rule version and classification values used at allocation/approval time.

When a rule or classification value changes:
- Show before/after configuration.
- Show categories and fields affected.
- Estimate the number of drafts, pending approvals, approved items and SAP-synchronized items potentially affected.
- Identify whether future creations only are affected or existing records would need migration.
- Require reason and appropriate approval.
- Do not rewrite approved records or SAP data automatically.
- Create a new rule version; preserve prior versions and audit history.
- Support rollback through a new controlled version/activation.
- Record activation time and activating actor.

J. DATA MODEL AND API REQUIREMENTS

Inspect the actual project schema before implementing. Reuse existing models and do not create duplicate configuration entities if equivalent entities already exist.

The implementation will likely need logical entities for:
- Classification dimension metadata.
- Classification values and relationships.
- Category-to-dimension configuration.
- Dimension-level rule configuration.
- Category + dimension override.
- Item Code rule set and ordered components.
- Item Description rule set and ordered components.
- Conditional rules and rule dependencies.
- Rule version, approval and activation history.
- Sequence allocation/counters, scoped and concurrency-safe.
- Rule test cases and validation results.
- Code allocation record / idempotency record.
- Rule impact assessment.
- Audit events.

Potential API capabilities, adapted to existing naming conventions:
- List/get classification dimensions.
- Create/update/retire classification values.
- Configure category field applicability and validation.
- Configure dimension-level defaults.
- Configure category + dimension overrides.
- Create/edit/clone Item Code rule sets.
- Create/edit/clone Item Description rule sets.
- Validate/simulate rules.
- Preview generated code and description without allocating a permanent code.
- Submit/approve/reject/activate rule versions.
- View version history and impact analysis.
- Allocate/reserve a permanent code through an authorized, idempotent server operation.
- View code-allocation history.
- Import/export classification values and rule configuration with validation.

All APIs must enforce RBAC, scope checks, optimistic concurrency or equivalent conflict detection, audit logging and server-side validation. Never expose a secret or permit an unauthorized user to activate rules.

K. ACCEPTANCE TESTS — REQUIRED BEFORE CLAIMING COMPLETION

1. An administrator can configure a separate Item Code and Item Description rule for each enabled category.
2. An administrator can configure each classification dimension/table and set its contribution to code and description independently.
3. An administrator can configure a category-specific override for a field/dimension without changing the reusable global default.
4. The configuration UI clearly shows inherited defaults, overrides, effective settings and rule version.
5. Adding an approved new classification value requires no frontend source-code change.
6. Adding a supported metadata-defined dimension does not require a frontend hardcoded dropdown change.
7. If a new physical database field is required, it goes through a reviewed migration rather than arbitrary runtime schema alteration.
8. Category selection dynamically changes visible fields, required fields, dependent options, Item Code preview and Item Description preview.
9. Item Code components can be reordered and transformed according to safe rules.
10. Item Description components can be reordered, conditionally included and formatted according to safe rules.
11. Empty optional components do not leave doubled separators or malformed output.
12. Required missing fields prevent validation/submission.
13. Duplicate codes are prevented under concurrent users and multiple application workers.
14. A preview does not consume a permanent sequence unless the approved policy explicitly says so.
15. The same idempotent allocation request cannot allocate multiple different permanent codes.
16. Rule validation detects invalid references, conflicts, cycles and length/uniqueness issues.
17. An unapproved or invalid rule cannot be activated.
18. Rule changes do not silently alter approved item codes/descriptions or existing SAP ItemCodes.
19. Audit/version history identifies who changed, approved and activated each rule and when.
20. Item code and description rule versions are traceable from the item record and SAP synchronization record.
21. SAP field limits and mappings are based on verified metadata/API contract, not assumptions.
22. Existing portal, approval, RBAC, audit and SAP integration functionality continues to work.
23. Tests are run in the actual environment and exact results are reported. Do not claim tests passed if they were not run.

L. PAGE-BY-PAGE IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW

Follow the existing page-by-page review and explicit acceptance process:
1. Inspect existing frontend, backend, database models, migrations, classification masters, code/description logic and current spreadsheets/configuration.
2. Produce a gap analysis showing existing functionality versus this requirement.
3. Propose the data model, configuration precedence, safe rule language, APIs, permissions and migration plan.
4. Present the first page's design and implementation scope.
5. Implement only the approved page and its necessary backend/schema/tests.
6. Run actual checks and report their exact results.
7. Show a real rendered preview if the environment supports it; never fabricate screenshots or claim an unrun test.
8. Ask for explicit accept/reject/revise before proceeding, unless the user expressly authorizes implementation in batches.
9. Preserve all previous requirements and all approved functionality.

Recommended page sequence:
- Page 1: Classification Dimension Catalogue.
- Page 2: Classification Value Management.
- Page 3: Category Configuration.
- Page 4: Item Code Rule Builder.
- Page 5: Item Description Rule Builder.
- Page 6: Conditional Rule Configuration.
- Page 7: Rule Test Bench / Preview.
- Page 8: Rule Approval and Version History.
- Page 9: Integration with Item Creation Form.
- Page 10: Impact Analysis, Audit and Operational Reporting.

FINAL INSTRUCTION

Item Code and Item Description must be driven entirely by active, approved, persisted configuration, not hardcoded source-code conditions. Every classification table/dimension must have an understandable rule-configuration experience, including how that dimension is validated, made available by category, and included or excluded from Item Code and Item Description. Support dimension-level defaults and category + dimension overrides. Preserve all existing master-prompt requirements and implement changes through reviewed, versioned, auditable configuration. Start by inspecting the existing implementation and reporting the gap analysis; do not claim the feature is implemented until the code, persistence, UI, APIs and tests have actually been completed and verified.

