NEW MANDATORY ADDENDUM — GST VERIFICATION, LEGAL NAME MATCH, AND SAVE BLOCKING
================================================================================

This addendum extends the existing Business Partner Master requirements. It does
not replace, shorten, or weaken any prior requirement. Implement this behavior
in the actual application and backend; do not provide only a visual mock-up.

1. REQUIRED USER WORKFLOW
- The Business Partner create/edit form must include a clearly visible “Verify GST”
  action.
- Before verification, show a neutral “Not verified” status and keep Save/Submit
  disabled.
- When the user clicks “Verify GST”, validate the entered GSTIN and retrieve/
  validate GST registration details using the configured authorized verification
  provider or trusted backend integration.
- Show the legal name returned by the verification source prominently as
  “GST Legal Name”. Do not invent, infer, or fabricate a legal name.
- Compare the submitted GSTIN and all required GST-linked business details against
  the authoritative verification response and the form fields, according to the
  configured matching rules. Include legal name and other required details such
  as registration status and relevant PAN/GSTIN relationships where available.
- Show the actual verification result and a clear reason for any mismatch. Do not
  expose sensitive provider credentials or raw internal errors.

2. STATUS INDICATORS
- VERIFIED: display a green check/tick icon and clear “Verified — details match”
  text only when the backend has confirmed every required check succeeded.
- FAILED/MISMATCH: display a red cross/tick-style failure indicator and clear
  “Verification failed — save blocked” text if the GSTIN is invalid, the GST
  record is not found, registration status fails the configured rule, the legal
  name or required details do not match, or the provider reports a mismatch.
- PENDING/UNAVAILABLE: if verification is pending, times out, errors, or the
  provider is unavailable, show a neutral/pending or red failure state with a
  useful explanation and keep saving blocked. Never treat a timeout, API error,
  unavailable provider, or unverified result as a successful green state.
- Use both icon and text; do not rely on color alone for accessibility.
- The displayed legal name must be clearly labelled as sourced from GST
  verification, distinct from the user-entered Business Partner Name.

3. STRICT SAVE GATE — FRONTEND AND BACKEND
- Disable Save, Save Draft where a saved business record is being created, Submit,
  and equivalent record-persistence actions until verification succeeds.
- A red failure indicator or a pending/unverified status must block persistence.
- Enforce this rule on the backend/API as well as in the frontend. Hiding or
  disabling a button alone is insufficient.
- On save, the backend must verify that a valid, successful verification record
  exists for the exact current values of all GST-relevant fields. Reject the
  request if there is no successful verification, the verification has expired
  under configured policy, or the form data differs from the values that were
  verified.
- Do not accept a client-supplied `verified=true` flag as proof. Store a
  server-controlled verification result, source/provider reference, verification
  timestamp, verified GSTIN, verified legal name, relevant normalized comparison
  values, result status, and safe diagnostic reason/audit metadata.
- Bind the verification result to the exact verified values (or a secure
  server-side hash/version of those values). Re-check this binding when saving.
- If GSTIN, legal name, PAN-linked data, registration status input, or any other
  configured GST-relevant field changes after verification, immediately invalidate
  the previous successful result, clear the green state, return to Not verified,
  and disable saving until verification is run again.
- Prevent race conditions: if the form changes while a verification request is in
  flight, discard the stale response and do not enable Save.
- Apply the same gate to create and edit operations. A previously verified record
  must be re-verified if any relevant data changes or policy requires revalidation.
- Record attempts and outcomes in the audit trail, including actor, timestamp,
  business partner/request ID where applicable, verification source/reference,
  result, and reason. Do not log credentials or unnecessary sensitive payloads.

4. MATCHING POLICY
- Normalize fields safely for comparison (for example, trim surrounding
  whitespace and apply case-insensitive comparison where appropriate), but do not
  silently remove meaningful legal-name differences or declare a fuzzy match
  successful without an explicitly approved business rule.
- Define which fields require exact matching and which can use an explicitly
  configured normalization rule. Display the authoritative GST legal name and
  the entered name side by side when useful to explain a mismatch.
- If the legal name does not match the required policy, mark verification failed
  and block save. Do not automatically overwrite the user's entered name without
  showing the authoritative name and obtaining the required user action.
- Keep format validation separate from live GST registration verification.
  A syntactically valid GSTIN alone must never result in a green verified status.
- Do not claim to access a government system unless a configured authorized
  provider actually returned the result. If no provider is configured, display
  “Verification service not configured” (or equivalent) and block saving.

5. USER EXPERIENCE
- Display field-level errors and a concise overall result.
- During verification, show a loading state and prevent duplicate concurrent
  requests.
- On success, show the GST legal name, source/status details available to the
  application, and the green verified state.
- On failure, show a red failure state and a specific actionable reason where
  safely available (for example: invalid GSTIN, no registration found, legal-name
  mismatch, required detail mismatch, inactive registration if prohibited by
  policy, provider unavailable).
- Keep Save disabled while failed. After the user corrects relevant fields, require
  a new verification attempt before enabling Save.
- Preserve the entered form data after a failed verification so the user can
  correct it; do not save the Business Partner record as a workaround.

6. ACCEPTANCE TESTS — ALL REQUIRED
A. New record with no verification: Save is disabled in UI and API rejects save.
B. Successful provider verification with matching details: authoritative GST Legal
   Name is displayed; green verified state appears; save succeeds only for the
   verified values.
C. Invalid GSTIN: red failure state; save blocked in UI and API.
D. GSTIN not found or required detail mismatch: red failure state; save blocked.
E. Legal-name mismatch: entered name and returned GST legal name are distinguishable;
   save remains blocked under the matching policy.
F. Provider timeout, outage, malformed response, or unconfigured provider: no green
   state; save remains blocked.
G. Change GSTIN or another GST-relevant field after successful verification: green
   state is cleared immediately; save becomes disabled; a new verification is
   required.
H. Submit a forged API request with `verified=true` but without a server-validated
   successful verification record: API rejects it.
I. Change the request values after verification and replay the old verification
   token/reference: API rejects it.
J. Two overlapping verification requests where an older response returns last:
   stale response cannot mark the current form verified.
K. Edit an existing Business Partner's GST-relevant details: the same rules apply.
L. Audit records are generated for success/failure without leaking secrets.
M. Keyboard/screen-reader users can distinguish verified, failed, and pending
   states without relying on color alone.

IMPLEMENTATION COMPLETION RULE:
Do not mark this requirement complete until the UI behavior, backend enforcement,
verification-source adapter, audit behavior, and applicable automated tests have
been implemented and tested. If the real GST verification provider is unavailable,
finish the provider adapter/configuration and failure-safe behavior, but clearly
report that live verification remains unverified. Never use fabricated GST data
or a simulated success response outside explicitly labelled automated tests.

END OF GST VERIFICATION ADDENDUM
