NEW MANDATORY ADDENDUM — MANAGE MISSING COUNTRY / STATE / DISTRICT / CITY / POSTAL VALUES
================================================================================

PURPOSE
Extend the existing Business Partner Master address/geography cascading feature.
If a required Country, State/Province, District, City, or Postal Code is missing
from the available list, authorized users must have a controlled way to request,
add, review, and activate the missing value. Do not force users to select an
incorrect location just because the correct value is unavailable.

1. CASCADING ADDRESS ORDER
Use this exact order:
Country → State/Province → District → City → Postal Code.

Each child list must be filtered by the selected parent. The interface must keep
the hierarchy consistent and must not allow an invalid parent-child combination.

2. ADD-MISSING-VALUE ACTION
For each geography selector, provide a clearly visible action such as:
“Can’t find the Country? Add / Request Country”
“Can’t find the State/Province? Add / Request State/Province”
“Can’t find the District? Add / Request District”
“Can’t find the City? Add / Request City”
“Can’t find the Postal Code? Add / Request Postal Code”

Use context-sensitive wording and only show the relevant action for the field.
The action should open a modal or inline form without unexpectedly clearing the
Business Partner form or previously entered address values.

3. REQUIRED FIELDS AND VALIDATION
- Country: country name and a stable unique country code (ISO code where applicable).
- State/Province: selected parent country, state/province name, and applicable
  stable code.
- District: selected parent state/province (and country), district name, and
  applicable code if available.
- City: selected parent district/state and country, city/locality name, and code
  if applicable.
- Postal Code: selected country and relevant parent geography (state/district/city
  as applicable), postal code, and optional locality/area description where useful.
- Required parent values must exist before a child value can be added. Do not allow
  orphan records or a city to be attached to the wrong district/state/country.
- Trim whitespace, validate required inputs, prevent duplicates after appropriate
  normalization, and display understandable validation errors.
- Where a national geography dataset does not use districts or a particular level,
  support a configured optional level without fabricating values. Preserve the
  required display order whenever the level is applicable.

4. GOVERNED CREATION AND IMMEDIATE USABILITY
- Respect the portal's existing RBAC and governance model.
- Authorized Geography Administrators may add/approve geography master values
  directly if their permissions allow it.
- Other users may submit a “Request Missing Geography” request for review.
- Provide configurable policy: direct add by authorized role, or request-and-approve.
  Default to controlled governance; do not grant every ordinary user permission to
  change shared master data.
- A newly added value must not become globally selectable until it passes required
  validation and approval, unless an authorized administrator's configured policy
  permits immediate activation.
- Once approved/activated, refresh the relevant dropdowns and make the value
  selectable without requiring the user to abandon and recreate the Business
  Partner form.
- Preserve the current form values and automatically select the new value only
  when the user explicitly confirms that choice and its parent hierarchy matches.
- If the missing parent itself is absent, guide the user through creating/requesting
  the parent first, then the child. For example, create/request the Country before
  its State/Province, and the State/Province before its District, then City and
  Postal Code.
- Include request status (Draft, Submitted, Approved, Rejected, Active or equivalent),
  requester, reviewer, timestamps, rejection reason, and audit history as applicable.
- Provide a Geography Master Administration page to search, add, edit, approve,
  activate/deactivate, and inspect country/state/district/city/postal mappings.
  Protect existing referenced values: do not hard-delete a geography value used by
  Business Partner records. Use deactivation/merge with a controlled migration path.

5. DATA MODEL AND API
Implement persistent geography master data and request workflow using the existing
project's architecture. Include:
- Stable ID/code, display name, level/type, parent ID, normalized comparison key,
  active status, source, created/updated metadata, and audit metadata.
- Uniqueness constraints appropriate to the level and parent (e.g., the same city
  name may exist under different districts; the same postal code may exist in more
  than one locality depending on country rules).
- Parent-child referential integrity, indexes, and safe database migrations.
- API endpoints/services for searching/selecting geography, submitting a missing
  value request, validating parent relationships, approving/rejecting, activating,
  and refreshing the relevant lookup data.
- Backend authorization on every mutation and approval route. UI-only permission
  hiding is insufficient.
- Safe concurrency handling so duplicate submissions cannot create duplicate
  active master values.
- Track source/provenance (trusted dataset, authorized administrator, user request,
  or approved import). Do not present user-entered values as government-verified
  unless independently verified by a trusted source.

6. BUSINESS PARTNER FORM INTEGRATION
- Integrate the feature into all relevant Business Partner address forms, including
  any applicable billing, shipping, and additional address sections.
- Preserve cascading order: Country → State/Province → District → City → Postal
  Code.
- When a parent changes, clear or revalidate all dependent child selections.
- When the user adds or requests a missing value, return them to the original
  address form with their other inputs intact.
- Do not bypass the mandatory GST verification/save gate. Geography creation or
  request approval does not itself verify GST and must not enable Business Partner
  saving while GST verification remains pending or failed.
- Validate the final address hierarchy before saving the Business Partner record.

7. ACCEPTANCE TESTS — REQUIRED
A. Each geography dropdown provides the correct add/request action when a value is
   unavailable.
B. A user without geography-admin permission cannot directly publish shared
   geography master data; they can submit a request if permitted.
C. An authorized administrator can add and activate a valid geography value.
D. Parent values are mandatory and the API rejects orphan child values.
E. Duplicate values under the same applicable parent are prevented safely.
F. A city with the same name in a different district can be represented where the
   geography model permits it.
G. Approving a request makes the value available in the correct dependent dropdown
   without losing the user's Business Partner form data.
H. Changing a parent invalidates incompatible child selections.
I. Referenced geography values cannot be hard-deleted; deactivation/merge is
   governed and audited.
J. API authorization, validation, referential integrity, and audit tests pass.
K. The mandatory GST verification save gate remains effective independently of
   geography management.
L. The feature is usable on supported screen sizes and has accessible labels,
   keyboard operation, and clear error/success states.

IMPLEMENTATION COMPLETION RULE
Build the actual UI, persistent data model, backend APIs, role checks, approval
workflow, audit history, and tests. Do not implement only a static “Add City”
button or keep newly added values only in frontend memory. If a trusted full
geography dataset is not available, implement the governed master and import path
and clearly label unverified/user-provided values; do not fabricate official data.

END OF GEOGRAPHY MANAGEMENT ADDENDUM
